   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to providing\nfor alternative penalties for a first-time violation of certain provisions of\nthe alcoholic beverage control law",
    "basePrintNo": "S7765",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7765-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§118 & 130, ABC L (as proposed in S.5979 & A.5972)",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7765\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to\nproviding for alternative penalties for a first-time violation of\ncertain provisions of the alcoholic beverage control law\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2021,\nc.738, changing which section of the Alcoholic Beverage Control (ABC)\nLaw is being amended and removing the changes to the section of Law\npreviously amended.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds to the definition of \"for cause\" in § 118 of the ABC Law\nto include failure to cure a violation as per subdivision six of this\nsection.\n\nSection 2 amends § 118 of the ABC Law by adding a new subdivision six,\nwhich gives the State Liquor Authority (SLA) the discretion to provide\nlicensees and permittees with a cure period for first time di minimis\nviolations of administrative processes, paperwork, or acts of omission,\ninstead of having to begin a disciplinary process. The cure period shall\nbe no shorter than 15 business days and no longer than 20 business days,\nunless a longer period is authorized by Law.\n\nThe SLA may not grant such a cure period or waiver of penalties for any\nviolations that may present an actual threat of harm to the public,\npresent a danger to public safety, health, or the environment; is a\nwillful violation, relates to a material portion of the licensee's busi-\nness; is similar to a previous violation; is a violation of the civil,\nhuman rights, tax, or penal law; results in the loss of employee wages\nor benefits, interferes with the review, remedy, resolution, of any\nclaims of discrimination or harassment; or is determined to be in\ncontravention of the public interest or against SLA mission.\n\nSection 3 removes amendments to subdivision three of § 130 of the ABC\nLaw, which gave the SLA the discretion to provide licensees or permit-\ntees with a cure period for di minimis violations, within limits.\n\nSection 4 revives subdivision 5 of § 130 of the ABC Law.\n\nSection 5 makes this act take effect on the same date and in the same\nmanner as a chapter of the laws of 2021 amending the Alcoholic Beverage\nControl Law relating to providing for alternative penalties for a first\ntime violation of certain provisions of the alcoholic beverage control\nlaw, as proposed in legislative bills numbers S.  5979 and A. 5972.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nNone.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation is a negotiated change to the underlying chapter.\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter amendment to Chapter 738 of the Laws of 2021. S.5979 passed the\nSenate in June 2021, and A.5972 passed the Assembly in March 2021.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as a\nchapter of the laws of 2021 amending the alcoholic beverage control law\nrelating to providing for alternative penalties for a first time\nviolation of certain provisions of the alcoholic beverage control law,\nas proposed in legislative bills numbers S.  5979 and A. 5972, takes\neffect.\n",
